vendor/kanwa/kanwa-readings.json holds character and compound readings from
the kanwa data of jitendex-data, the data behind the Jitendex dictionary.
Hachidori uses them to place headword furigana over the kanji each reading
belongs to where the KANJIDIC2 readings in vendor/kanjidic/ cannot: 日本 にほん
as 日(に)本(ほん), and compounds such as 今日 きょう read as a whole inside a
longer word.

  jitendex-data: https://github.com/Jitendex/jitendex-data
  Jitendex: https://jitendex.org/, by Stephen Kraus

The readings were taken from kanwa/characters.json and kanwa/compounds.json
at the jitendex-data commit that vendor/kanwa/source.json records, with the
SHA-256 of each file. Changes: readings are converted to hiragana; a
kun'yomi keeps the "." before its okurigana; the "-" that marks a prefix or
suffix reading is removed; a character's Chinese and Korean readings, its
empty readings and the readings KANJIDIC2 already gives it are left out, as
are kana characters and compounds that contain kana or have one character; a
repeated reading is listed once.

